  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Antandroy\"\nsubtitle: \"Madagascar\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Tandroy, a traditionally nomadic people |\n| Where | The arid far south of Madagascar, in the Androy Region |\n| How many | About 600,000 in 2013 |\n| Language | A dialect of Malagasy, a Malayo-Polynesian language |\n| Religion | Ancestor veneration combined with Christianity (mostly Lutheran) |\n| Known for | Plank-built wooden houses · Spear dances with shrill whistles · Hand-spun cotton weaving with spiritual power · Painted tombs crowned with zebu skulls · Ironsmithing and firearms repair |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Tandroy are the fifth-largest ethnic group in Madagascar, living in the dry southern region whose name means 'place of the thorns'. They are traditionally pastoral, herding zebu cattle, and farm drought-resistant crops like maize and manioc. They stand out from other Malagasy peoples through their distinctive wooden architecture, sacred cotton textiles, and elaborate funeral rituals centered on zebu sacrifice.\n\n## Material culture\n\nTandroy material culture reflects their arid homeland and pastoral economy, with wood, metal, raffia, and zebu byproducts forming the basis of houses, tools, containers, and ornaments.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nWomen hand-spin cotton and weave it into cloth, with the yarn holding spiritual power before it is finished.\n\n- **Living yarn** (*fole velo*) — Hand-spun thread imbued with spiritual power before stiffening, used in circumcision ceremonies and tied around the wrist by healers for protection.\n- **Lamba wrapper** (*lamba*) — Woven cotton wrapper, off-white with geometric designs in blue and red silk on the short ends and fringed.\n- **Raw silk** (*landy*) — Raw silk used for clothing and as patterning yarn on cotton loincloths.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nTandroy dress is minimal because of the hot climate, centered on woven cotton wrappers and loincloths with metal ornaments.\n\n- **Woven cotton loincloth** — Off-white cotton with blue and red silk geometric designs on short ends and fringe.\n- **Basketry hat** — Hat woven of basketry worn as part of daily dress.\n- **Goat-hair hat-and-wig** — Hat combined with wig made of goat hair as hair adornment.\n- **Hide sandal** — Sandal made of cattle hide, worn in pairs.\n\n### Architecture\n\nThe Tandroy build distinctive square houses of wooden planks on stilts, with doors assigned by gender and family role.\n\n- **Plank house** — Square house with vertically hung planks on a wooden frame, raised on low stilts, peaked roof, no windows, and three gendered doors—front for women, rear for children, third for men.\n- **Tomb** (*fanesy*) — Large rectangular structure sized to the dead man's wealth, painted in colors, with stones or towers on either side and zebu skulls on the roof.\n- **Yard fence** (*raketa*) — Fence of prickly-pear cactus or cut indigenous succulents from the spiny forest.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nThe Tandroy are renowned ironsmiths, making weapons and tools, while containers and utensils are carved from wood or woven from raffia and gourd.\n\n- **Wood-and-metal spear** — Spear combining wood and metal, a weapon and object of prestige.\n- **Iron axe** — Axe with wooden handle and iron blade.\n- **Gourd milk container** — Gourd used to store and serve zebu milk and curd, dietary staples.\n- **Raffia basket** — Lidded basket woven of raffia for holding jewelry, medicinal plants, or money.\n- **Carved wooden spoon** — Spoon carved with a figure.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nMetal ornaments, especially silver, are the primary form of adornment, worn in multiples with goat-hair wigs and sacred protective thread.\n\n- **Silver necklace** — Necklace of silver, a prestige metal and sign of wealth.\n- **Metal bracelet** — Bracelets worn in multiples as adornment.\n- **Metal hair-pin** — Pin used to dress and secure hair.\n- **Sacred protective thread** (*fole velo*) — Living yarn tied around the wrist by healers for protection and ritual purposes.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nStringed instruments dominate Tandroy music, accompanied by shrill whistles and fipple flutes in dances and ritual contexts.\n\n- **Box zither** (*marovany*) — Box zither built from pine planks and strung with unwound bicycle cables.\n- **Chordophone with frets** (*mandolina or gitara*) — Popular southern chordophone strung with nylon fishing line with five or seven movable frets to shift tuning.\n- **Bowed fiddle** (*lokanga*) — Bowed instrument with carved gourd resonator making a three-stringed fiddle.\n- **Trance state** (*bilo*) — State in which the living commune with and are possessed by ancestors.\n\n## Dance & theatre\n\nThe Tandroy are known across Madagascar for distinctive spear dances accompanied by shrill whistles, with special performances at funerals.\n\n- **Spear dance** — Distinctive dance performed with spears, accompanied by shrill whistles and fipple flutes.\n- **Funeral coffin ceremony** — Celebratory performance at some funerals where men lift the coffin and run with it into the sea.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nTandroy ritual life centers on life-cycle rites and ancestor veneration, with circumcision and funerals as the major ceremonies.\n\n- **Circumcision ceremony** — Central male rite in which the foreskin may be fired from a gun barrel and fole velo thread is used for protection.\n- **Funeral feast and zebu sacrifice** — Communal gathering where zebu are killed and meat distributed to the community, repeated at coffining and tomb completion.\n- **Tomb completion ceremony** — Two-day mourning ritual during fanesy tomb construction with ritual wailing and possible zebu sacrifice.\n- **House burning** — The deceased's house is burned to close funeral rites, and the family does not visit the tomb again.\n- **Prohibitions** (*fady*) — Rules set by elders and ancestors that govern daily life, including that women cannot milk zebu and the radiated tortoise cannot be killed.\n\n## Foodways\n\nThe arid climate means the Tandroy eat less rice than other Malagasy, depending instead on drought-resistant crops and zebu products.\n\n- **Staple grains and tubers** — Maize, sweet potato, manioc, yams, taro root, and millet, generally boiled and sometimes served in milk or with peanuts.\n- **Zebu milk and curd** — Dairy products that are dietary staples and stored in gourd containers.\n- **Honey** — Major Tandroy product from the region.\n- **Ceremonial beef** — Zebu meat from sacrificed animals at funerals and other rituals, distributed to the community.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nOral history splits by clan, with some groups claiming ancient settlement and others tracing refugee origins, and descent narratives define clan identity.\n\n- **Karimbola clan tradition** — Western Tandroy clan with no migration story, implying they were always in place.\n- **Mahandrovato tradition** — Eastern Tandroy original population gradually outnumbered by Antanosy and Bara refugees.\n- **Zafindravoay descent** — Clan claiming descent from a union of a Tandroy woman and a crocodile.\n- **Spirit being** (*kokolampo*) — Spirit understood to act for good or ill in a person's life.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nThe Tandroy speak a Malagasy dialect related to Borneo languages and practice ancestor veneration combined with Lutheran Christianity.\n\n- **Creator god and ancestor cult** — Religion combines belief in a creator god with veneration of ancestors who live on, intervene, and must be respected.\n- **Trance communion** (*bilo*) — State in which the living commune with and are possessed by ancestors.\n- **Soothsayer** (*mpisoro*) — Village figure with recognized authority in traditional communities.\n- **Healer** (*ambiasa*) — Practitioner who works with consecrated fole velo thread for protection and healing.\n- **Lutheranism** — Dominant Christian form among the Tandroy since late 19th century, coexisting with older ancestral beliefs.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *fole velo* — Living yarn, hand-spun thread imbued with spiritual power before stiffening\n- *lamba* — Woven cotton wrapper garment\n- *landy* — Raw silk used for clothing and patterning\n- *fanesy* — Tomb or eternal place of the dead\n- *raketa* — Prickly-pear cactus used for yard fences\n- *marovany* — Box zither stringed with bicycle cables\n- *mandolina* — Chordophone with movable frets, strung with fishing line\n- *gitara* — Another name for mandolina chordophone\n- *lokanga* — Bowed three-stringed fiddle with carved gourd resonator\n- *bilo* — Trance state in which the living commune with ancestors\n- *fady* — Prohibition or taboo set by elders and ancestors\n- *mpisoro* — Soothsayer with recognized authority in village society\n- *ambiasa* — Healer who works with sacred thread and protective amulets\n- *kokolampo* — Spirit that acts for good or ill in a person's life\n- *zebu* — Humped cattle, central to Tandroy economy and ritual\n- *Tandroy* — People of the thorns, from the spiny forest of southern Madagascar\n- *Androy Region* — Arid southern region of Madagascar between Amboasary and Beloha\n- *Malagasy* — Language of Madagascar, Malayo-Polynesian family from Borneo\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- Acquier, Jean-Louis. Architectures de Madagascar. Berlin: Berger-Levrault, 1997.\n- Olson, James. The Peoples of Africa: An Ethnohistorical Dictionary. Westport, CT: Greenwood Publishing, 1996.\n- Condra, Jill. Encyclopedia of National Dress: Traditional Clothing Around the World. Los Angeles: ABC-Clio, 2013.\n- Raison-Jourde, Françoise, and Solofo Randrianja. La nation malgache au défi de l'ethnicité. Paris: Karthala, 2002.\n- Ron Emoff, \"Music of Madagascar: An Overview\", in Ellen Koskoff (ed.), The Concise Garland Encyclopedia of World Music. New York: Routledge, 2008, pp. 123–129\n- Museum of the Tandroy, Berenty Reserve, north of Taolagnaro\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antandroy\n- https://folkways.si.edu/search?query=Madagascar\n- British Museum online collection: https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection\n\n",
